**Theorem 14.1 (eight compatible groups on the entire supplied face).**
Under Assumption 10.1 and the additional conditions

$$
R_-\succeq0,\qquad a_o^2+b_o^2>0,\qquad
w_o^TR_+w_o\ge0,
\tag{FC.48}
$$

the complete contact partition $\mathscr G$ has at most eight
groups. Every multiplier-optimal common law can be replaced by
one with at most eight supported-nonzero low labels, preserving
its full $\Gamma$ moment vector, achieved $K$ and actual $J_t$.
For the entire supplied multiplier face,

$$
U_8=[r_-(t,\lambda),r_+(t,\lambda)],\qquad
n_t(r)\le8\quad(r\in[r_-,r_+]).
\tag{FC.49}
$$

This includes single-point contrast intervals, zero weights,
zero heights, repeated branch zeros and singular semidefinite matrices.

Proof. Each pure contact belongs to its own group, possibly paired
with its saturated endpoint. Theorem 12.2 bounds these groups by
five. By Theorem 13.1 every other group is a positive-height
saturated singleton with $x^TR_+x<0$. By Theorem 13.2 all these
singletons have the same report sign $\epsilon$ and height sign
$\sigma=+1$. They are zeros of one and the same complete
$F_{1,\epsilon,+}$, so Theorem 12.3 bounds them by three.
Therefore the entire partition, including unused contacts, has
at most $5+3=8$ groups. The two numbers arise from whole-circle
zero restrictions and their common coefficients, not from adding
five live coordinates to three failure coordinates.

For completeness about the quantifier over laws, integrate (FC.5)
against any multiplier-optimal law. Its nonnegative gap has zero
integral, so the law is on the full contact set. Theorem 3.3,
using (FC.8), says that any occupied interior fraction splits into
its two contact endpoints with weakly same-sign heights. Opposite
strict signs with two positive split masses would incur a positive
triangle defect and cannot occur. Split the entire law and collect
its endpoint masses in each of the at most eight complete groups.
For a paired group of masses $a_0,a_1$ the replacement has
$p=a_0+a_1$, $\xi=a_1/p$ if $p>0$; for $p=0$ omit it.
For a singleton keep its total mass. The same group operation
works for the admitted integral laws by their group masses and
first fraction moments, as in Theorem 3.3. It preserves every
component of $\Gamma$, $K$, and, by the original weak-sign
merge identity (FC.9), $J_t$ exactly. Thus this is a replacement
for every law, not a bound on the labels in every unmerged instrument.

Theorem 4.2 gives the complete face's compact contrast interval and
activity $K=r$ at each original optimal inequality request in it.
Applying the just-established replacement to any law at each such
$r$ gives $n_t(r)\le8$. The exact owner criterion Theorem 5.2
then gives (FC.49). Equivalently all vertices and edges of the
complete $\mathcal P$ use at most these eight groups, so the
same criterion covers their whole contrast image. This uses the
existing criterion without a new face, rank, or Farkas argument.
$\square$

